    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "Recreating NES Final Fantasy (1990 USA) with KGraph Parsing & Automated Playthroughs",
      "dcterms:description": "Step-by-step engineering tutorial walking through how we collected the Final Fantasy (NES USA) reference folder in ~/Downloads/ff1, parsed it into a 1,206-node Knowledge Graph, extracted missing 6502 ROM combat and map tables, built the Godot 4 runtime, and verified the Coneria-to-Garland playthrough with Cucumber video recordings.",
      "robos:topic": "JRPG Knowledge Graph Ingestion, 6502 ROM Tables & Godot 4 Cucumber Verification",
      "robos:difficulty": "Advanced",
      "robos:level": "Advanced",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Game Engineers, Reverse Engineers & RobOS AI Agent Developers",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "120 minutes",
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:targetApplication": "urn:robos:library:robos-jrpg-parser",
      "robos:documentationPage": "docs/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough.md",
      "robos:webUrl": "/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough/",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:modulesCount": 6,
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-04T19:15:00.000Z",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "mod-01-browser-and-python-asset-acquisition",
          "title": "Module 1: Collecting the ~/Downloads/ff1 Reference Folder with a Reusable Harvester",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Walk through how we assembled /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1 from VGMaps, StrategyWiki, the NES manual PDF, NSF/MP3 audio archives, and Disch’s 6502 disassembly using packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py, and why inspecting PNG IHDR dimensions early reveals a critical difference between the Overworld map and town/dungeon sheets.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">1. Why a Single Walkthrough Scrape Is Never Enough</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            When we started recreating the 1990 North American NES release of <em>Final Fantasy</em> in <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1</code>, the first question was what raw material the parser actually needs on disk. A single text walkthrough gives you a sequence of story events and maybe a weapon price table, but it tells you nothing about where walls or doors sit on a map, what palette colors draw a treasure chest, or how many hit points a Fighter gains at Level 2.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            To build a complete Knowledge Graph without committing copyrighted Nintendo files into the public Git repository, we keep all raw reference material in a private local folder (<code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1</code>) organized from five complementary sources: VGMaps 1:1 pixel map PNGs (<code>FinalFantasy-*.png</code>), StrategyWiki HTML sub-pages (<code>strategywiki/Final_Fantasy-*.html</code>), the 84-page Nintendo instruction booklet (<code>CLV-P-NAAAE.pdf</code>), the NES sound driver and soundtrack rips (<code>Final Fantasy (1987-12-18)(Square).nsf</code> and <code>Final_Fantasy_OST_MP3.zip</code>), and Disch's commented 6502 NES disassembly (<code>nes_disassembly/</code>).\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">2. Extracting a Manifest in the Browser vs. Running <code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code></h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            If you hardcode thirty <code>curl</code> commands for <em>Final Fantasy</em> filenames, your script is useless the next time you want to parse <em>Dragon Warrior</em> or <em>Chrono Trigger</em>. Instead, we wrote <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code> so you only pass three parameters: the VGMaps console index page (either a live URL like <code>https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm</code> or a saved local copy like <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/vgmaps-index.html</code>), a filename prefix (<code>--map-prefix \"FinalFantasy-\"</code>), and a StrategyWiki guide slug (<code>--wiki-slug \"Final_Fantasy\"</code>).\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Note why the script parses <code>&lt;a href=\"...\"&gt;</code> anchor links rather than <code>&lt;img src=\"...\"&gt;</code> tags: on VGMaps console index pages, the full-resolution 1:1 pixel rips are linked from table anchors, whereas inline images are either absent or small icons. If you are inspecting an unfamiliar archive in Chrome or Firefox first, you can preview the exact same link list in DevTools Console (<code>F12</code>) before running Python:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>// Optional browser check in DevTools Console (F12) on https://vgmaps.com/Atlas/NES/index.htm\n(() =&gt; {\n  const prefix = \"FinalFantasy-\";\n  const maps = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"a[href]\")]\n    .map(a =&gt; new URL(a.getAttribute(\"href\"), location.href).href)\n    .filter(u =&gt; u.split(\"/\").pop().startsWith(prefix) &amp;&amp; u.toLowerCase().endsWith(\".png\"))\n    .map(url =&gt; ({ url, file: decodeURIComponent(url.split(\"/\").pop()) }));\n  console.log(`Discovered ${maps.length} map PNGs`, maps.slice(0, 5));\n})();</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            In normal use, you do not even need to open DevTools—<code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code> runs the same link discovery via Python's built-in <code>html.parser.HTMLParser</code>, writes <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/sources-manifest.json</code>, fetches any missing files using a standard desktop browser <code>User-Agent</code> header (because VGMaps and Zophar reject Python's default <code>Python-urllib/3.x</code> header with <code>HTTP 403 Forbidden</code>), and writes <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/download-receipt.json</code>.\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># Run the reusable harvester against ~/Downloads/ff1\npython3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1 \\\n  --vgmaps-index /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/vgmaps-index.html \\\n  --map-prefix \"FinalFantasy-\" \\\n  --wiki-slug \"Final_Fantasy\"</code></pre>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">3. What the PNG <code>IHDR</code> Dimensions in <code>download-receipt.json</code> Tell Us</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            As <code>harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code> verifies each file, it unpacks bytes <code>16..24</code> of every PNG's <code>IHDR</code> header to log its exact width and height. Looking at the console output on <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1</code> immediately reveals something important:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>[DISCOVER] Wrote /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/sources-manifest.json (30 map PNGs, 14 wiki pages)\n[CACHED    ] FinalFantasy-World.png                         334817 B (4096x4096)  sha256=4f33f06704a3\n[CACHED    ] FinalFantasy-World(Unmarked).png               450234 B (4096x4096)  sha256=ccd0cbe5bd52\n[CACHED    ] FinalFantasy-Coneria.png                        11518 B (987x544)    sha256=c1cd05b20629\n[CACHED    ] FinalFantasy-ConeriaCastle.png                  15685 B (1127x640)   sha256=1940b26947c2\n[CACHED    ] FinalFantasy-TempleOfFiends(Present).png        11670 B (805x594)    sha256=d684eca4ff34</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Why is <code>FinalFantasy-World(Unmarked).png</code> an exact <code>4096&times;4096</code> square (<code>256&times;256</code> tiles of <code>16&times;16</code> pixels), whereas <code>FinalFantasy-Coneria.png</code> is <code>987&times;544</code> and <code>FinalFantasy-TempleOfFiends(Present).png</code> is <code>805&times;594</code>? Because on VGMaps, the contributor cropped out the repeating outer water/forest padding around towns, placed title banners at the bottom, and arranged side rooms or multiple castle floors onto a single composite image. Those PNGs are great visual references for our Knowledge Graph and clean-room art generator, but you cannot just divide <code>987&times;544</code> by <code>16</code> and call it a <code>64&times;64</code> walkability grid. In Module 4, we will see how we pair these VGMaps sheets with the 6502 ROM's RLE-compressed <code>64&times;64</code> map banks.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">4. Handing the Folder to a RobOS Agent (<code>jrpg-parse-assets</code>)</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Once the files are in <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1</code>, you can ask your RobOS Agent in <strong>RobOS Agent Chat</strong> (or your active CLI: <code>agy</code>, <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, or <code>copilot</code>) to parse the folder. The agent loads the repository skill <code>.agents/skills/jrpg-parse-assets/SKILL.md</code> and runs <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js</code> through <code>scan</code>, <code>parse</code>, and <code>coverage</code>.\n          </p>\n          <div class=\"doc-figure\">\n            <img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/jrpg-acquisition-and-rom-pipeline.png\" alt=\"Stage 1: Browser and Python Multi-Source JRPG Asset Acquisition Pipeline\" class=\"doc-img\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\">\n            <div class=\"doc-caption\">Figure 1.1: Stage 1 pipeline — discovering VGMaps and StrategyWiki links into sources-manifest.json, verifying SHA-256 hashes and PNG dimensions in ~/Downloads/ff1, and invoking robos-jrpg-parser.</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run `harvest_jrpg_assets.py --discover-only` against `/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/vgmaps-index.html` to generate `sources-manifest.json`",
            "Run `harvest_jrpg_assets.py` to verify all 44 cached map PNGs and StrategyWiki HTML pages and inspect `download-receipt.json`",
            "Run `jrpg-parser.js scan /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1` to see how the classifier assigns roles and automatically skips GBA/PSX remake files",
            "Run `jrpg-parser.js parse /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1 --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes` to build the Knowledge Graph"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "First, run `harvest_jrpg_assets.py` in `--discover-only` mode so it parses `/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/vgmaps-index.html` for `FinalFantasy-*.png` links and builds the 14 StrategyWiki sub-page URLs into `sources-manifest.json`. Then inspect the generated JSON with `jq`.",
