          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "In Disch’s 6502 NES disassembly of Final Fantasy, why does the Masamune have a 40/201 (~19.9%) base critical hit rate while the Vorpal sword has a lower critical rate than later weapons in the inventory table?",
              "options": [
                "Because critical hit rates on the NES are dynamically derived from (Weapon ATK - Target DEF) clamped to the 1..40 range before rolling 0..200",
                "Because the weapon struct in Bank 06 omits the critical rate byte, so the combat routine reads the weapon’s 0-based accuracy modifier divided by 2 as the critical threshold",
                "Because the NES PPU frame counter seeds the RNG with the weapon’s equip-slot memory offset plus the wielder’s Luck stat divided by 4",
                "Because a pointer bug in the combat routine loads the weapon’s 1-based Inventory Index ID (1 to 40) instead of its critical rate byte from the weapon table"
              ],
              "answer": "Because a pointer bug in the combat routine loads the weapon’s 1-based Inventory Index ID (1 to 40) instead of its critical rate byte from the weapon table",
              "explanation": "Although the weapon data table in the ROM has a byte intended for critical rate, a pointer bug in the 6502 combat routine uses the weapon’s 1-based Inventory Index ID (1..40) out of 201 instead (Nunchaku = 1/201, Rapier = 6/201, Masamune = 40/201)."
            },
            {
              "question": "When a Black Mage casts FIRE (Effectivity/Power E=10, Spell Accuracy=24) against an IMP (Magic Defense M.D=16, neither weak nor resistant to Fire), how does the NES magic routine determine whether the spell deals normal (10..20) or doubled (20..40) damage?",
              "options": [
                "It starts with Base Magic Accuracy 148 (0x94), adds Spell Accuracy (24), and subtracts Target M.D (16) for a threshold of 156; rolling 0..155 on rand(0..200) scores a magic \"hit\" that doubles the rand(E..2E) roll",
                "It compares Caster Intelligence + Spell Accuracy (24) against Target M.D (16) on a d100 roll; if successful, the base damage E is multiplied by the caster’s current spell level",
                "It rolls rand(0..255) against (128 + Caster Luck - Target EVA); magic defense (M.D) only applies to status spells like SLEEP and DARK rather than elemental damage spells",
                "It calculates (Spell Power * 2) - Target M.D directly as flat deterministic damage and only rolls accuracy if the target has an active elemental resistance flag"
              ],
              "answer": "It starts with Base Magic Accuracy 148 (0x94), adds Spell Accuracy (24), and subtracts Target M.D (16) for a threshold of 156; rolling 0..155 on rand(0..200) scores a magic \"hit\" that doubles the rand(E..2E) roll",
              "explanation": "In the NES ROM (Intelligence is unused by the magic formula!), base magic accuracy is 148 (0 if resistant, 188 if weak to the element). Chance to hit = Base + Spell Accuracy - Target M.D = 148 + 24 - 16 = 156 on a 0..200 roll (where 200 always misses). A hit doubles the E..2E damage roll."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does robos:JRPGEnemy in our NES Knowledge Graph copy the monster Evade byte into Agility, and how is monster turn order computed at the start of each combat round?",
              "options": [
                "Monsters always act after all four Light Warriors in descending order of their 0-based formation slot index (0..8)",
                "The NES ROM does not store a separate monster Agility byte; a monster’s Evade (EVA) stat serves as both its physical evasion percentage and its turn-order initiative base (EVA + rand(0..49))",
                "Monsters use their Morale (MOR) stat divided by 2 plus rand(0..49) for initiative, while Evade (EVA) only affects physical dodge rolls",
                "Monster initiative is derived from (ACC + Hits * 10) + rand(0..63), which is why high-hit bosses always act first"
              ],
              "answer": "The NES ROM does not store a separate monster Agility byte; a monster’s Evade (EVA) stat serves as both its physical evasion percentage and its turn-order initiative base (EVA + rand(0..49))",
              "explanation": "While player characters have both Agility (AGL) and Evade (EVA), the 20-byte NES monster record stores only EVA, which doubles as physical evasion and turn-order initiative base (EVA + rand(0..49))."
            }
          ]
