        {
          "id": "mod-04-tile-grid-slicing-and-nes-palettes",
          "title": "Module 4: Slicing 16×16 Tile Grids, Decoding RLE Map Banks & Fixing the Indoor Ceiling Palette",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Understand why VGMaps town/dungeon PNGs (987×544, 805×594) had to be paired with the 6502 ROM’s RLE-compressed 64×64 map streams (bank_04.dat..bank_07.dat), how tile properties map walkability and Coneria’s entrance column (x=16), and why lut_SMPalettes painted indoor rooms and treasure chests solid white (0x30) until we applied the +0x20 inside-room palette.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">1. Slicing VGMaps Atlases vs. Decompressing the <code>64&times;64</code> RLE Map Banks</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            In Module 1, we noticed that <code>FinalFantasy-Coneria.png</code> is <code>987&times;544</code> pixels and <code>FinalFantasy-TempleOfFiends(Present).png</code> is <code>805&times;594</code> pixels. In <code>build_ff1_resources.py</code> (lines <code>515–565</code>), we still slice every <code>FinalFantasy-*.png</code> into <code>16&times;16</code> cells, deduplicate identical tiles by their RGBA byte buffer, and save the unique tileset strips and grid indices into <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/vgmaps_16x16/</code>.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            However, for the game engine to know the exact <code>(x, y)</code> tile coordinates of every wall, door, shop, staircase, NPC, and treasure chest on the NES hardware's <code>64&times;64</code> map coordinate system, we also decompress the 61 standard maps directly from ROM banks <code>bank_04.dat..bank_07.dat</code> (lines <code>597–630</code> of <code>build_ff1_resources.py</code>) and join each tile ID (<code>0..127</code>) with its 2-byte property definition in <code>bank_00.dat</code> (<code>offset 0x0800 + tilesetId * 256</code>):\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># RLE decompression of all 61 64x64 standard maps from bank_04..bank_07 in build_ff1_resources.py:\nfor map_id in range(61):\n    ptr = struct.unpack_from(\"&lt;H\", b4567, map_id * 2)[0]\n    pos = ((ptr &gt;&gt; 14) &amp; 0x03) * 0x4000 + (ptr &amp; 0x3FFF)\n    raw = bytearray()\n    while pos &lt; len(b4567) and len(raw) &lt; 4096:\n        b = b4567[pos]; pos += 1\n        if b == 0xFF: break\n        elif b &amp; 0x80:\n            run = b4567[pos]; pos += 1\n            raw.extend([b &amp; 0x7F] * (256 if run == 0 else run))\n        else:\n            raw.append(b)\n    grid = [list(raw[r*64:(r+1)*64]) for r in range(64)]</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            This produces <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/map_grids/map_00_coneria.json</code> through <code>map_60_titan_s_tunnel.json</code>. Each file contains the exact <code>64&times;64</code> integer grid, the 128-tile <code>tileDefinitions</code> dictionary (<code>walkable</code>, <code>teleportType</code>, <code>specialType</code>, <code>argByte</code>), and the resolved <code>chests</code>, <code>teleports</code>, <code>doorsShopsAndTriggers</code>, and <code>npcs</code> arrays.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">2. Why Spawning at <code>(15, 22)</code> in Coneria Trapped the Hero in a Tree</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Having <code>map_00_coneria.json</code> on disk immediately solved our first gameplay bug. When we first wired up the transition from <code>PartyCreate.tscn</code> into <code>Coneria.tscn</code>, we guessed a spawn coordinate of <code>Vector2i(15, 22)</code> near the bottom gate. When we watched the recorded MP4 video, the hero ended the video stuck inside a green tree!\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Inspecting <code>map_00_coneria.json</code> around rows <code>y=18..23</code> and columns <code>x=14..18</code> showed why right away: Coneria's south entrance is a single-column walkable brick street at <strong>column <code>x=16</code> (<code>tileId=16</code>, <code>walkable: true</code>, matching the ROM's <code>lut_EntrTele_X = 16</code>)</strong>, bordered immediately on both the left (<code>x=15</code>) and right (<code>x=17</code>) by unwalkable trees (<code>tileId=14</code>, <code>walkable: false</code>). Moving the spawn coordinate one tile right to <code>Vector2i(16, 21)</code> placed the hero squarely on the brick road.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">3. Why Indoor Rooms and Treasure Chests Initially Rendered as Solid White Squares</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            To render exact <code>1024&times;1024</code> (<code>64&times;64</code> tiles &times; <code>16&times;16</code> pixels) map textures for Godot (<code>ff1-game/assets/maps/coneria.png</code>, <code>coneria_castle_1f.png</code>, <code>coneria_castle_2f.png</code>, <code>temple_of_fiends.png</code>), we drew each map's <code>64&times;64</code> grid using the ROM's <code>2&times;2</code> TSA metatiles, 2bpp CHR graphics, and the map's palette entry in <code>lut_SMPalettes</code> (<code>$A000</code> in <code>bank_00.dat</code>, offset <code>0x2000 + map_id * 0x30</code>, 48 bytes per map).\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            On our first render of <code>temple_of_fiends.png</code>, the entire interior of the northwest treasure room—and the treasure chests at <code>(2, 3)</code> and <code>(3, 3)</code>—came out as solid white squares! Looking at the 48-byte entry for Map 12 in <code>lut_SMPalettes</code> explained why:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># lut_SMPalettes in bank_00.dat (offset 0x2000 + map_id * 0x30):\n# Bytes  0..15 (+0x00): Outside-Room Background Palettes -> Palette 0 is [0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] (ALL WHITE!)\n# Bytes 16..31 (+0x10): Sprite Palettes\n# Bytes 32..47 (+0x20): Inside-Room Background Palettes  -> Palette 0 is [0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F] (Black, Silver, Brown!)\n\nmap_pal     = list(b00[0x2000 + map_id * 0x30 : 0x2000 + map_id * 0x30 + 16])\nin_room_pal = list(b00[0x2000 + map_id * 0x30 + 0x20 : 0x2000 + map_id * 0x30 + 0x30])\nif map_pal[1] == 0x30 and map_pal[2] == 0x30 and map_pal[3] == 0x30:\n    map_pal[0:4] = in_room_pal[0:4]  # Restores [0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F] for indoor rooms &amp; chests!</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            On the NES, indoor rooms and treasure chests use <code>tsa_attr == 0</code> (Palette 0). When you are walking outside a room, the game loads the Outside-Room palette where Palette 0 is <code>[0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30]</code> so the room interior is hidden behind a solid white roof ceiling. When you step through a doorway onto an indoor tile, the PPU swaps in the Inside-Room palette at <code>+0x20</code> (<code>[0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F]</code>), revealing the floor and painting the treasure chest metatiles in black outline (<code>0x0F</code>), silver iron trim (<code>0x10</code>), and wood brown (<code>0x17</code>).\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
