            {
              "description": "Inspect the extracted frames and final game state from Scenario 2 (`speak_to_coneria_townsfolk_and_the_king_of_coneria_in_the_throne_room.mp4`).",
              "code": "jq '{scene: .scene_shown.name, hero_position, king_intro_done: .game_state.king_intro_done, last_dialog: .dialog_box.last_text}' \\\n  /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game/tests/e2e/reports/gamestate_speak_to_coneria_townsfolk_and_the_king_of_coneria_in_the_throne_room.json",
              "expected": "The report shows the party in `ConeriaCastle2F` at `{\"x\": 12, \"y\": 13, \"facing\": \"up\"}` in front of the King of Coneria with `king_intro_done: true`.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/labs/lab-m6-s3-scenario2-king-throne.png",
              "imageAlt": "Extracted video frames from Scenario 2 showing Coneria townsfolk dialogue and the King of Coneria in ConeriaCastle2F",
              "caption": "Lab 6.3: Scenario 2 extracted frames — speaking to Coneria townsfolk and receiving the rescue mission from the King in ConeriaCastle2F.",
              "title": "Inspect Scenario 2 (`speak_to_coneria_townsfolk_and_the_king_of_coneria_in_the_throne_room.mp4`) and its `gamestate_*.json` report"
            },
            {
              "description": "Extract frame `0.5s` and the final victory frame from Scenario 3 (`open_a_treasure_chest_in_the_temple_of_fiends_and_defeat_garland.mp4`) and inspect the final game state JSON.",
              "code": "cd /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game\nffmpeg -y -ss 0.5 -i tests/e2e/reports/videos/open_a_treasure_chest_in_the_temple_of_fiends_and_defeat_garland.mp4 \\\n  -frames:v 1 /tmp/verify_chest.png\njq '{opened_chests: .game_state.opened_chests, inventory: .game_state.inventory, garland_defeated: .game_state.garland_defeated, princess_rescued: .game_state.princess_rescued, bridge_built: .game_state.bridge_built}' \\\n  tests/e2e/reports/gamestate_open_a_treasure_chest_in_the_temple_of_fiends_and_defeat_garland.json",
              "expected": "Frame `0.5s` (`/tmp/verify_chest.png`) shows the genuine wooden-and-silver NES treasure chests at `(2, 3)` and `(3, 3)` in front of the hero at `(3, 4)` with `\"In the treasure chest, the LIGHT WARRIORS found HEAL Potion!\"`, and the final state confirms `garland_defeated: true, princess_rescued: true, bridge_built: true`.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/labs/lab-m6-s4-scenario3-chest-and-garland.png",
              "imageAlt": "Extracted video frames from Scenario 3 showing authentic silver/brown NES treasure chests at (2,3) and (3,3) and defeating Garland",
              "caption": "Lab 6.4: Scenario 3 extracted frames — authentic silver-and-brown NES treasure chests at (2, 3) & (3, 3) and defeating Garland in turn-based combat.",
              "title": "Extract frame `0.5s` and the final battle frame of Scenario 3 (`open_a_treasure_chest_...mp4`) to verify the silver-and-brown NES chests at `(2, 3)` and `(3, 3)` and Garland’s defeat"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "In Scenario 3 (open_a_treasure_chest_in_the_temple_of_fiends_and_defeat_garland.mp4), all Gherkin assertions for opening the chest and receiving a Heal Potion passed, yet the chest rendered as a solid teal box. What pair of interacting bugs caused that visual artifact?",
              "options": [
                "temple_of_fiends.png was rendered with Outside-Room Palette 0 ([0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] white ceiling) AND FF1MapScene.gd drew a semi-transparent ColorRect debug marker (_chest_markers) over chest coordinates that blended with the white tile into a solid teal square",
                "The treasure chest CHR tile IDs (0x2A, 0x2B, 0x3A, 0x3B) were accidentally mapped to Coneria Castle’s water fountain metatiles in lut_Tileirse",
                "The Xvfb virtual framebuffer was started at 8-bit color depth (-screen 0 768x720x8) and FFmpeg’s yuv420p chroma subsampling quantized brown pixels into teal",
                "Godot’s CanvasItem modulate property on the TileMap layer was set to Color(0, 1, 1, 1) whenever a chest contained a consumable item"
              ],
              "answer": "temple_of_fiends.png was rendered with Outside-Room Palette 0 ([0x0F, 0x30, 0x30, 0x30] white ceiling) AND FF1MapScene.gd drew a semi-transparent ColorRect debug marker (_chest_markers) over chest coordinates that blended with the white tile into a solid teal square",
              "explanation": "Two issues compounded: first, Outside-Room Palette 0 filled the chest tile with solid white (0x30); second, FF1MapScene.gd placed a semi-transparent colored ColorRect (_chest_markers) over every chest tile, which alpha-blended against the white square into a flat teal box. Switching to Inside-Room Palette 0 (+0x20: [0x0F, 0x10, 0x17, 0x0F]) and removing the ColorRect revealed the genuine silver-and-brown NES chest pixel art."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why was the Cucumber assertion Then the current map scene should be \"Coneria\" insufficient on its own to catch the Coneria tree-spawn defect in Scenario 1, and what closed that verification gap?",
              "options": [
                "Because Coneria.tscn and TempleOfFiends.tscn share the same scene_file_path string in Godot 4",
                "Because /api/v1/state only verified the scene name (\"Coneria\") without checking whether hero_position landed on a walkable brick road tile (x=16, tileId=16) or visually inspecting the final MP4 frame extracted via ffmpeg -sseof -0.3 -frames:v 1",
                "Because GameControlServer.gd returns cached state from TitleScreen.tscn until at least 10 seconds of real-clock time have elapsed",
                "Because behave skips all Then steps whenever Godot runs with --fixed-fps 60 inside a container"
              ],
              "answer": "Because /api/v1/state only verified the scene name (\"Coneria\") without checking whether hero_position landed on a walkable brick road tile (x=16, tileId=16) or visually inspecting the final MP4 frame extracted via ffmpeg -sseof -0.3 -frames:v 1",
              "explanation": "Checking only the scene name (\"Coneria\") passed even when the hero spawned on an unwalkable border tree at (15, 22). Combining active movement steps (\"When the hero moves up 3 tiles\" to (16, 18)) with FFmpeg key-frame extraction (ffmpeg -sseof -0.3 ... -frames:v 1) verifies both walkability rules and visual rendering."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does the Cucumber test harness launch Godot with --write-movie <avi_path> --fixed-fps 60 inside Xvfb :98 and transcode the AVI to H.264 MP4 afterward, rather than capturing the X11 display with ffmpeg -f x11grab?",
              "options": [
                "Because x11grab cannot record windows rendered with the GL Compatibility OpenGL backend on Linux",
                "Because Xvfb :98 does not allocate a virtual framebuffer in RAM unless --write-movie is passed on the Godot command line",
                "Because --write-movie --fixed-fps 60 captures every rendered viewport frame and mixed audio buffer deterministically inside the engine loop regardless of host CPU load, whereas x11grab drops or duplicates frames under CI load",
                "Because --write-movie automatically generates the Gherkin step definitions and JSON-LD Knowledge Graph nodes from the video stream"
              ],
              "answer": "Because --write-movie --fixed-fps 60 captures every rendered viewport frame and mixed audio buffer deterministically inside the engine loop regardless of host CPU load, whereas x11grab drops or duplicates frames under CI load",
              "explanation": "Godot’s built-in Movie Maker (--write-movie with --fixed-fps 60) steps the simulation by a fixed 1/60s delta per frame and writes both exact viewport pixels and synchronized audio to the AVI stream before transcoding to H.264 MP4, eliminating wall-clock jitter and dropped frames."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "@id": "urn:robos:package:learning",
  "@type": [
    "oslc:ServiceProvider",
    "robos:Package"
  ],
  "dcterms:description": "Interactive developer courses, tutorials, and architectural training modules.",
  "dcterms:title": "Interactive eLearning Curriculums & Knowledge Modules",
  "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
  "robos:nodes": [
    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:getting-started-with-robos",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:description": "Your first hour with RobOS: what it is, how to install only the apps you need, where your data lives and how to back it up, how to change an app by describing it, and how to use AI features safely or not at all. Short lessons, hands-on steps and quizzes that explain every answer.",
      "dcterms:title": "Getting Started with RobOS",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "45 minutes",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "body": "## Three things you do with RobOS\n\n1. **Use** ready-made apps: Point of Sale for a shop or café, Auto Center for a repair shop, Household for family finances, a media library, games, smart-home control and a full developer suite.\n2. **Customize** any app by describing the change you want. You do not need to read its code.\n3. **Build** a new app from a description, the same way the suites were built.\n\n## Why it works: the knowledge graph\n\nEvery RobOS app is described in a **knowledge graph** (KGraph): what the app is, how it works and how to test it. That written-down description is what lets an AI agent change an app safely, and what lets this eLearning app show you courses. You will meet the KGraph again in lesson 4.\n\n## What RobOS is not\n\n| It is | It is not |\n|---|---|\n| Free, open source (Apache-2.0) | A subscription |\n| Your data on your machine | A cloud service that holds your data |\n| AI as an optional extra | An app that stops working without AI |\n\n> The long-term plan: keep and reuse what AI builds, so each new app needs **less** AI, not more.",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "id": "gs-01-what-robos-is",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Use, customize, build: the three ways to work with RobOS.",
            "Every app is described in the knowledge graph, which is what makes changes safe.",
            "Apps run on your computer and work with no AI configured."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open rowbose.com and read the two-move plan under **The vision**.",
            "Pick one app you pay for today and find its RobOS replacement in the App Directory at rowbose.com/apps/."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS is one open-source app suite that replaces the apps you pay for, one at a time, on your own computer. This lesson gives you the map before you install anything.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The app's description in the knowledge graph (KGraph)",
              "explanation": "The KGraph records what the app is, how it works and how to test it. A change starts as an edit to that description.",
              "options": [
                "A cloud copy of your data that the agent edits",
                "The app's description in the knowledge graph (KGraph)",
                "A paid RobOS support plan",
                "A screenshot of the app you send to the agent"
              ],
              "question": "What lets an AI agent change a RobOS app without you reading its code?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "It works normally; AI features are optional extras",
              "explanation": "Every RobOS app must stay fully usable with no agent configured. AI is always behind an explicit action you choose to take.",
              "options": [
                "It opens in a read-only demo mode",
                "Only the developer apps keep working",
                "It works normally; AI features are optional extras",
                "It asks you to subscribe before it starts"
              ],
              "question": "What happens to a RobOS app if you never set up an AI agent?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 1: What RobOS is, and what it is not"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Get the App Manager\n\nDownload RobOS from **rowbose.com/download/**. Pick the file for your computer:\n\n- **Windows**: the setup `.exe`.\n- **Ubuntu GNOME**: the `.deb`. Double-click it, or run `sudo apt install ./RobOS-*-Linux-amd64.deb`.\n- **Other Linux desktops**: the AppImage (needs FUSE 2).\n- **macOS**: the DMG for Intel or Apple Silicon.\n\nThe base download holds the manager and one shared runtime. Apps are separate downloads, so you only pay for what you install.\n\n## Install apps\n\n1. Open **RobOS App Manager**.\n2. Expand a group and tick the apps you want. Ticking a group selects all of its apps.\n3. Check the download size, then choose **Install selected**. Shared pieces download once.\n\nEach app gets its own launcher. On Ubuntu GNOME they appear in a **RobOS Apps** folder in the app grid; on macOS under `~/Applications/RobOS Apps`.\n\n## Remove apps\n\nTick installed apps and choose **Uninstall selected**. **Uninstall RobOS** removes the manager and every app it installed. Your personal settings in `~/.config/robos/` are kept either way.",
          "durationMinutes": 8,
          "id": "gs-02-install",
          "keyPoints": [
            "One manager, then pick only the apps you need.",
            "Uninstalling keeps your settings in ~/.config/robos/."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Download the RobOS App Manager for your operating system from rowbose.com/download/.",
            "Install one app you will really use, for example **RobOS Household** or **RobOS eLearning**.",
            "Launch it from its own launcher, not from the App Manager."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS installs as one small manager, the RobOS App Manager, and you download only the apps you want from it.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "They stay in ~/.config/robos/",
              "explanation": "Removing apps or the whole manager keeps ~/.config/robos/, so reinstalling picks up where you left off.",
              "options": [
                "They stay in ~/.config/robos/",
                "They are deleted with the apps",
                "They are uploaded to rowbose.com first",
                "They move to the Trash"
              ],
              "question": "You uninstall RobOS from the App Manager. What happens to your personal app settings?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Apps are separate downloads you choose in the App Manager",
              "explanation": "The manager ships with one shared runtime. Each app downloads only when you select it, and shared pieces download once.",
              "options": [
                "Only a trial version is included",
                "Apps are separate downloads you choose in the App Manager",
                "The apps are compressed until you pay",
                "Apps stream from the cloud while you use them"
              ],
              "question": "Why is the base download small?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 2: Install RobOS and your first apps"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Local first\n\nApp settings and data live under `~/.config/robos/` on your machine. Nothing is sent to a RobOS server, because there isn't one.\n\nPasswords and API keys are kept in **pass**, the standard Unix password store, encrypted with your GPG key. You can add a YubiKey or another FIDO2 key for extra protection.\n\n## Back up with Config Sync\n\nOpen **RobOS Preferences → Config Sync & Sharing** and point it at a folder in a cloud drive you already use. You can create two kinds of target:\n\n- A **vault**: a full backup of your settings. It syncs each file both ways, shows you conflicts and keeps restore points.\n- A **share**: a read-only bundle of selected settings, for example a product catalog, that someone else can merge into their own lists.\n\nCredentials are stripped by name and are **never** uploaded, in either case.\n\n```text\nyour cloud folder/\n  robos-config.json   ← what is in the backup\n  blobs/              ← the file contents\n```",
          "durationMinutes": 7,
          "id": "gs-03-your-data",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Your data is files under ~/.config/robos/ on your computer.",
            "A vault is a full backup; a share is a read-only bundle for someone else.",
            "Passwords and keys never leave pass, and are never uploaded."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open `~/.config/robos/` in your file manager and find the folder of an app you installed.",
            "Open **RobOS Preferences → Config Sync & Sharing** and connect a vault to a folder in your cloud drive.",
            "Change one setting in an app, then check that the vault shows the change."
          ],
          "overview": "RobOS keeps your data in files on your own computer. Config Sync lets you back it up, or share part of it, through a cloud folder you already own.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "A share",
              "explanation": "A share is a read-only bundle of selected settings that the other person merges into their own lists. A vault is your full private backup.",
              "options": [
                "A restore point",
                "A pass entry",
                "A vault",
                "A share"
              ],
              "question": "You want a friend to get your café menu but not the rest of your settings. Which Config Sync target do you use?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Strips them and never uploads them",
              "explanation": "Credentials are removed by key name before anything is written to the cloud folder.",
              "options": [
                "Strips them and never uploads them",
                "Uploads them only to shares",
                "Encrypts them and uploads them to the vault",
                "Asks you each time"
              ],
              "question": "What does Config Sync do with credentials such as API keys?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 3: Where your data lives, and how to back it up"
        },
        {
          "body": "## The flow\n\n1. **Describe the change.** For example: *Add an allowance envelope for each kid that tops up every Friday and shows on the accounts page.*\n2. **The knowledge graph changes first.** RobOS plans the change as new and changed nodes, such as `Envelope`, `TopUpSchedule` and a new test, before any code is written.\n3. **An agent builds it on a branch.** Your current app is untouched while it works.\n4. **Tests and a walkthrough prove it.** The agent runs the app headlessly and hands back a narrated walkthrough of the new feature.\n5. **You decide.** Keep the change, or throw it away.\n\n## Which agent?\n\nRobOS uses the coding agent you already have: Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI. You sign in to it once and choose it as the active agent. RobOS runs it for you.\n\n## Why the graph comes first\n\nBecause the change is written down before it is built, you can review *what* will change in plain terms, and the tests that come with it check the result. That is the difference between describing a change and hoping an AI got it right.",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "id": "gs-04-customize",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Describe → graph edit → agent builds on a branch → tests and walkthrough → you keep or discard.",
            "Nothing changes in your working app until you keep the change.",
            "You bring your own coding agent."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Write down one small change you would like in an app you use (one sentence is enough).",
            "Read the **Build apps** guide at rowbose.com/build-apps/ and find the step where the knowledge graph is edited.",
            "If you have a coding agent installed, open **Agents Manager** and check that it shows up."
          ],
          "overview": "In RobOS you change an app by saying what you want. The request becomes an edit to the app's knowledge graph, an agent builds it, and tests prove it before you keep it.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The app's knowledge graph",
              "explanation": "The request becomes an edit to the KGraph first, so the change is planned and reviewable before an agent writes code.",
              "options": [
                "The app's code on your main branch",
                "The App Manager",
                "The app's knowledge graph",
                "Your saved data"
              ],
              "question": "What is the first thing that changes when you ask for a new feature?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Throw the change away; your app is unchanged",
              "explanation": "The agent works on a branch. You only keep the change if the tests and walkthrough convince you.",
              "options": [
                "Restore your computer from a backup",
                "Reinstall the app",
                "Nothing; the change is already live",
                "Throw the change away; your app is unchanged"
              ],
              "question": "An agent finished your change and the walkthrough looks wrong. What do you do?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "The ones you already use, such as Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI",
              "explanation": "RobOS has no model of its own to sell you. It runs the agent CLI you sign in to.",
              "options": [
                "The ones you already use, such as Claude Code, Codex, GitHub Copilot or Gemini CLI",
                "Only a RobOS-hosted model",
                "None; you must write the code yourself",
                "Only agents bought through RobOS"
              ],
              "question": "Which coding agents can RobOS run for you?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 4: Change an app by describing it"
        },
        {
          "body": "## How AI shows up in an app\n\nAn AI feature sits behind an explicit action: a text box with a **✦** button that starts disabled until you type. Nothing is sent until you press it. You pick which agent answers with the agent selector next to it.\n\nExamples:\n\n- **Audio Player**: describe a mood and an AI Playlist picks and orders songs, or turn on the Virtual DJ, which writes short intros between songs.\n- **Household**: ask questions about your own statements.\n\nLeave these alone and the apps work as ordinary apps.\n\n## The Prompt Security Guard\n\nBefore any prompt reaches an agent, RobOS checks it for secrets (cloud keys, tokens, private keys), personal data (card numbers, SSNs) and prompt-injection attempts. You choose what it does when it finds something:\n\n| Mode | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `redact` | The secret is replaced before sending |\n| `block` | The prompt is not sent |\n| `warn` | You are warned and can decide |\n| `audit-only` | It is sent and logged |\n| `off` | No checks |\n\nFindings are logged in `~/.config/robos/prompt-security-audit.json`.\n\n## Local models\n\nIf you would rather not use a cloud agent at all, RobOS can answer one-shot requests with a local model through Ollama. That API mode is **off by default**; you turn it on in RobOS Preferences.",
          "durationMinutes": 8,
          "id": "gs-05-ai-safely",
          "keyPoints": [
            "AI features start only when you press ✦.",
            "You choose the agent for each feature.",
            "The Prompt Security Guard checks every prompt before it is sent."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open RobOS Preferences and find where the Prompt Security Guard mode is set.",
            "Pick the mode you are comfortable with (`redact` is a sensible start).",
            "Optional: try an AI feature such as an AI Playlist, then look for its entry in the audit log."
          ],
          "overview": "Some RobOS apps have optional AI features, like the Audio Player's Virtual DJ. You always start them yourself, you choose the agent, and a prompt guard checks what is sent.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "The key is replaced before the prompt is sent",
              "explanation": "Redact mode removes the secret and sends the rest. Block mode would stop the prompt entirely.",
              "options": [
                "The app closes",
                "The key is replaced before the prompt is sent",
                "The prompt is sent as typed and logged",
                "The key is sent but encrypted"
              ],
              "question": "Your prompt accidentally contains an AWS secret key and the guard is in redact mode. What happens?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Don't press its ✦ buttons; there is nothing else to switch off",
              "explanation": "AI only runs when you start it. An app with no agent configured is a normal app.",
              "options": [
                "Disconnect from the internet",
                "Uninstall the app's AI plug-in",
                "Don't press its ✦ buttons; there is nothing else to switch off",
                "Cancel a RobOS AI subscription"
              ],
              "question": "How do you stop an app from ever calling AI?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 5: Use AI features safely, or not at all"
        },
        {
          "body": "## Where courses come from\n\nCourses are `robos:ELearning` nodes in the **learning** package of the knowledge graph (`.robos/kgraphs/learning/package.jsonld`). Each lesson has a summary, lesson text, key ideas, lab steps and quiz questions with explanations.\n\n## Edit a course\n\nOpen a course and choose **Edit course**. Use the **⋯** menu on any lesson to edit it, add a lesson after it or delete it. Then **Save course** writes it back to the knowledge graph and to `.robos/elearning.yaml`.\n\n## Share a course\n\n- **Export website** turns the course into a static HTML page you can host anywhere, for example GitHub Pages.\n- **Export as HTML Zip** on a lesson packages that one lesson for offline use.\n- The same exporter runs from a terminal:\n\n```bash\nnode packages/robos-elearning/cli-export-web.js --course=getting-started-with-robos --output=course.html\n```\n\n## Have an agent write one\n\nThe `generate-app-elearning` agent skill reads an app from the knowledge graph and drafts a course for it: lessons, labs and quizzes. Review it here, edit what is wrong and save.\n\n## Your progress and certificate\n\nProgress is saved on this computer in `~/.config/robos/elearning/progress.json`, so you can stop at any time. Finish every lesson, lab and quiz to get a certificate you can save as a PDF.",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "id": "gs-06-keep-learning",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Courses are knowledge-graph nodes you can edit and save.",
            "Export a course as a website, or one lesson as an offline zip.",
            "Progress is saved locally; finishing earns a PDF certificate."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Turn on **Edit course** in this course and open the **⋯** menu on this lesson.",
            "Choose **Copy as Markdown** and paste it somewhere to see the lesson's source.",
            "Finish this course and save your certificate as a PDF."
          ],
          "overview": "Every course in this app lives in the knowledge graph, so you can edit one, export it as a website, or have an agent write a course for any app.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "As a node in the learning package of the knowledge graph",
              "explanation": "Courses are robos:ELearning nodes in .robos/kgraphs/learning/package.jsonld, which is why agents and people can edit them the same way.",
              "options": [
                "As a node in the learning package of the knowledge graph",
                "In a RobOS cloud account",
                "Inside the eLearning app's code",
                "In your browser's local storage"
              ],
              "question": "Where is a course stored?"
            },
            {
              "answer": "Export website",
              "explanation": "Export website produces a self-contained HTML page. Copy course path and Copy KGraph URI are references for AI agents.",
              "options": [
                "Save as PDF",
                "Export website",
                "Copy course path",
                "Copy KGraph URI"
              ],
              "question": "You want to put a course on your team's website. What do you use?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lesson 6: Keep learning, and write your own course"
        }
      ],
      "robos:modulesCount": 6,
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:targetAudience": "New RobOS users: shop owners, families, players and developers",
      "robos:topic": "RobOS basics: install, data, customizing apps and optional AI",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-03T00:00:00Z"
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:app:customer-portal",
      "@type": [
        "robos:ELearning",
        "oslc:Resource",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:description": "Interactive deep-dive into Customer Portal SPA, covering its TypeScript / React architecture, API contracts, BDD test scenarios, and live operational execution.",
      "dcterms:title": "Customer Portal SPA Masterclass & Architecture Walkthrough",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "45 minutes",
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "durationMinutes": 15,
          "id": "mod-01-customer-portal",
          "labSteps": [
            "Inspect Customer Portal SPA node definition and contract declarations in Knowledge Graph",
            "Verify owner team routing (frontend-guild) and repository (github.com/acme/customer-portal)",
            "Review component catalog-info.yaml and service topology interfaces"
          ],
          "overview": "Understand the system design, tech stack (TypeScript / React), and dependencies of Customer Portal SPA.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "System component managed under team frontend-guild utilizing TypeScript / React",
              "explanation": "Customer Portal SPA is governed under the frontend-guild team topology utilizing TypeScript / React.",
              "options": [
                "Stateless API gateway proxy managed by platform-infra that only terminates TLS and forwards raw TCP frames",
                "System component managed under team frontend-guild utilizing TypeScript / React",
                "Background batch ETL worker scheduled via cron to archive historical billing records into cold storage",
                "Embedded SQLite storage engine running inside the cluster ingress controller"
              ],
              "question": "What is the primary architectural role of Customer Portal SPA?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Module 1: Architecture & Topology of Customer Portal SPA"
        },
        {
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "id": "mod-02-customer-portal",
          "labSteps": [
            "Explore API specifications and schema invariants for Customer Portal SPA",
            "Execute automated Gherkin verification scenarios adhering to strict Red-Green-Refactor cycle",
            "Inspect input validation and error payloads"
          ],
          "overview": "Explore schemas, contracts, and Gherkin verification scenarios governing Customer Portal SPA.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": "Through declarative contracts and BDD Gherkin test traceability",
              "explanation": "RobOS couples W3C SHACL and OpenAPI/Gherkin contract testing for total verification.",
              "options": [
                "Through declarative contracts and BDD Gherkin test traceability",
                "By relying solely on browser-side TypeScript compile checks without server contract validation or E2E scenarios",
                "By sampling 1% of production HTTP responses and logging schema mismatches to stdout",
                "By disabling strict JSON schema validation in CI so frontend and backend teams can deploy independently"
              ],
              "question": "How does Customer Portal SPA enforce schema and behavioral guarantees in RobOS?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Module 2: Contracts, APIs & Behavior-Driven Testing"
        },
        {
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "id": "mod-03-customer-portal",
          "labSteps": [
            "Execute local Test Fabric mock dispatch to simulate outbound dependencies",
            "Verify GitOps state synchronization in .robos/elearning.yaml and package definitions",
            "Attain 100% quiz score to earn your verifiable Certificate of Completion"
          ],
          "overview": "Verify GitOps state synchronization in .robos/elearning.yaml and earn your completion certificate.",
          "quiz": [
            {
              "answer": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
              "explanation": "All RobOS eLearning curriculums serialize declaratively to .robos/elearning.yaml.",
              "options": [
                "Inside git hook shell scripts under .git/hooks/post-commit",
                "Inside unversioned CI runner environment variables injected at container startup",
                "Inside ephemeral browser localStorage keys that remain local to each developer workstation",
                ".robos/elearning.yaml"
              ],
              "question": "Where are declarative course definitions stored for GitOps synchronization in RobOS?"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Module 3: Verification, Deployment & GitOps Lifecycles"
        }
      ],
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:targetApplication": "urn:robos:app:customer-portal",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Software Engineers & Platform Architects",
      "robos:topic": "Customer Portal SPA Systems Architecture",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-09-22T16:17:14.638Z"
