          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "When jrpg-parser.js parse invokes robos-lib/ai-agent on unstructured walkthrough prose to extract story milestones and battle rules, what mechanism prevents the LLM from injecting training-set memories (such as GBA Dawn of Souls bonus dungeons or hallucinated spell names) into knowledge-graph.jsonld?",
              "options": [
                "Requiring every agent-returned JSON record to carry a verbatim quote field and programmatically verifying that the exact substring exists in the local source file before accepting the node",
                "Running a secondary critic LLM pass that cross-checks every extracted node against an embedded SQLite database of NES ROM strings",
                "Lowering the model temperature to 0.0 and passing a JSON Schema with additionalProperties: false so only valid schema keys can be emitted",
                "Restricting the AI agent to only read HTML <table> elements while discarding all <p> and <li> narrative paragraphs before prompt construction"
              ],
              "answer": "Requiring every agent-returned JSON record to carry a verbatim quote field and programmatically verifying that the exact substring exists in the local source file before accepting the node",
              "explanation": "Temperature 0.0 and JSON Schema constraints enforce structure but cannot stop an LLM from recalling facts from other editions in its pretraining corpus. Requiring a verbatim quote and checking sourceText.includes(record.quote) guarantees every extracted fact is grounded in the local file."
            },
            {
              "question": "You scraped StrategyWiki’s Final Fantasy/Enemies page along with the official NES manual PDF, yielding 124 robos:JRPGEnemy nodes with HP, XP, Gold, and elemental weaknesses. Why does jrpg-parser.js coverage still flag Enemies and Encounters as \"Blocks battles\"?",
              "options": [
                "Because StrategyWiki lists monster names using the PlayStation Origins translation rather than the 6-character NES ASCII strings required by the SHACL validator",
                "Because summary wiki tables omit internal combat bytes—specifically ATK, ACC, Hits per turn, CRT, DEF, EVA, Magic Defense (M.D), Morale (MOR), AI spell lists, and the 8-slot per-map encounter formation tables",
                "Because the NES combat engine requires 3D skeletal animation hitboxes before it can resolve turn-based damage formulas",
                "Because the PDF manual parser cannot extract integer columns unless OCR post-processing is enabled via Tika gRPC"
              ],
              "answer": "Because summary wiki tables omit internal combat bytes—specifically ATK, ACC, Hits per turn, CRT, DEF, EVA, Magic Defense (M.D), Morale (MOR), AI spell lists, and the 8-slot per-map encounter formation tables",
              "explanation": "Basic wiki bestiaries and manuals list player-facing rewards (HP, XP, Gold) but omit the internal combat stats (ATK, ACC, Hits, CRT, DEF, EVA, M.D, MOR) needed to compute hit rolls, damage, initiative, and flee checks, as well as the 8-slot map formation tables."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does jrpg-parser.js separate scan (plus an optional manifest.json override) from parse when ingesting a multi-source ~/Downloads/<game-id> folder?",
              "options": [
                "Because Node.js cannot read HTML and PNG files in the same process without first generating an inode manifest on disk",
                "Because scan uploads the directory listing to the RobOS Cloud Vault to reserve globally unique @id URNs before local parsing begins",
                "Because mixed scrape folders often include remake/port pages (e.g., GBA Dawn of Souls or PSP Anniversary) whose altered shop prices and bonus bosses would pollute the NES KGraph unless inspected and excluded before parsing",
                "Because scan pre-compiles all PNGs into Godot .ctex textures so parse can read their pixel buffers directly from .godot/imported/"
              ],
              "answer": "Because mixed scrape folders often include remake/port pages (e.g., GBA Dawn of Souls or PSP Anniversary) whose altered shop prices and bonus bosses would pollute the NES KGraph unless inspected and excluded before parsing",
              "explanation": "Guides and wikis frequently mix NES original tables with GBA/PS1/PSP remake tables on sub-pages. Running scan first lets the agent inspect inferred roles and edition warnings and write manifest.json overrides ({ \"use\": false }) before parse merges data into the graph."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-03-nes-rom-tables-and-battle-quirks",
          "title": "Module 3: Extracting the Missing 6502 ROM Tables & Resolving NES Combat Bugs",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "Walk through /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py and update_reference_guide.py: how we extracted all 128 monster structs, 8-slot encounter tables, 12 class growth tables, and 64 spell bytes from Disch’s 6502 disassembly, verified the 3 famous NES battle bugs, and updated robos-jrpg-parser to build the 1,206-node graph.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">1. Verifying the Battle Rules Against Disch's 6502 Disassembly</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            In our initial gap audit, the parser had extracted 37 battle formulas from guide prose, but flagged that the guide had no named author and contained several suspicious rules. Cross-referencing those formulas against <strong>AstralEsper's NES Game Mechanics Guide</strong> (<code>v0.85</code>, GameFAQs ID <code>57009</code>) and <strong>Disch's commented 6502 NES disassembly</strong> (<code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/nes_disassembly/</code>) confirmed that the guide was describing three real bugs in the original 1990 NES cartridge:\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>1. The Weapon Critical Hit Pointer Bug:</strong> Every weapon record in <code>0C_8000_weapondata.bin</code> has a byte intended for critical hit rate, but the combat routine in Bank 0C never reads it. Instead, due to a pointer offset bug in <code>LoadOneCharacterIBStats</code>, the game loads the weapon's <strong>1-based Inventory Index ID (<code>1</code> to <code>40</code>)</strong> as its base critical hit rate out of <code>201</code>. Thus the Wooden Nunchuck (<code>ID 1</code>) has a <code>1/201</code> crit rate, the Rapier (<code>ID 6</code>) has a <code>6/201</code> crit rate, and the Masamune (<code>ID 40</code>) has a <code>40/201</code> (<code>~19.9%</code>) crit rate. Unarmed Black Belts and Masters use <code>Level * 2</code>.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>2. The Level-Up HP Formula:</strong> At every level-up, a character gains <code>floor(VIT / 4) + 1</code> base HP. Each class's 49-level growth table in <code>0B_9094_levelupdata.bin</code> also has a \"Strong HP\" flag (bit <code>0x20</code>); on Strong HP levels, the character gains an extra <code>20 + rand(0..5)</code> (<code>20</code> to <code>25</code>) HP.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong>3. Base 148 Magic Hit Chance &amp; Bugged Spells:</strong> A caster's Intelligence stat is completely ignored by the NES magic routine. Base magic accuracy is <code>148</code> (<code>0x94</code>)—or <code>0</code> if the target resists the spell's element, or <code>188</code> (<code>148 + 40</code>) if the target is weak to the element. The hit threshold is <code>Base + Spell Accuracy - Target Magic Defense</code> on a <code>0..200</code> roll (where <code>200</code> always misses). For offensive damage spells like <code>FIRE</code> or <code>LIT</code>, scoring a magic \"hit\" doubles the base <code>rand(E..2E)</code> damage roll. Furthermore, four spells in <code>0C_81E0_magicdata.bin</code> are famously broken in the NES assembly: <code>LOCK</code> always misses, <code>LOK2</code> accidentally adds <code>+20</code> to the target's Evade instead of subtracting it, and <code>TMPR</code> and <code>SABR</code> fail to increase Attack.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">2. Extracting All Binary Tables with <code>build_ff1_resources.py</code></h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Rather than hand-typing tables for a handful of opening-area monsters, we wrote <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py</code> to unpack the binary ROM tables in <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/nes_disassembly/bin/</code> directly into structured JSON in <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/</code>:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># Unpacking all 128 20-byte monster records from 0C_8520_enemydata.bin in build_ff1_resources.py:\nenemies_out = []\nfor i in range(128):\n    off = i * 20\n    exp, gp, hp = struct.unpack_from(\"&lt;HHH\", b_enemies, off)\n    morale, ai_id, evade, absorb, num_hits, hit_rate, damage, crit_rate, unk_e, atk_ail, cat, mag_def, weak, resist = b_enemies[off+6:off+20]\n    enemies_out.append({\n        \"id\": i + 1, \"romIndex\": i, \"name\": enemy_names[i],\n        \"hp\": hp, \"xp\": exp, \"gold\": gp,\n        \"attack\": damage, \"accuracy\": hit_rate, \"hits\": num_hits, \"critRate\": crit_rate,\n        \"defense\": absorb, \"evade\": evade, \"agility\": evade,  # Evade doubles as initiative!\n        \"magicDefense\": mag_def, \"morale\": morale,\n        \"categories\": decode_bits(cat, CATEGORIES),\n        \"weaknesses\": decode_bits(weak, ELEMENTS),\n        \"resistances\": decode_bits(resist, ELEMENTS),\n        \"statusAttackAilment\": decode_bits(atk_ail, AILMENTS),\n        \"ai\": ai_scripts[ai_id] if ai_id &lt; len(ai_scripts) else None\n    })</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Notice line 10 above: <code>\"evade\": evade, \"agility\": evade</code>. Because the 20-byte NES monster struct has no separate Agility byte, the battle engine uses <code>evade + rand(0..49)</code> for monster turn-order initiative. Setting both fields in <code>enemies.json</code> and in the Knowledge Graph ensures any downstream combat engine knows how to sort turn order.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            In the same run, <code>build_ff1_resources.py</code> extracts all 128 battle formations and the 8-slot weighted encounter tables (<code>12/64, 12/64, 12/64, 12/64, 6/64, 6/64, 3/64, 1/64</code>) into <code>nes_data/encounters.json</code>; all 12 classes, their Level 1 starting stats (from <code>bank_00.dat</code> at <code>$B040</code>), and their 49-level stat/MP growth bitmasks into <code>nes_data/classes_and_xp.json</code>; all 64 magic spells and 26 enemy special attacks into <code>nes_data/spells.json</code>; and all 256 treasure chests, 232 map NPCs, and 256 DTE-decoded dialogue strings into <code>nes_data/chests_and_npcs.json</code> and <code>nes_data/dialogues.json</code>.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">3. Merging the Tables into the 1,206-Node Knowledge Graph</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Finally, <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/update_reference_guide.py</code> formats those extracted JSON tables into Markdown sections in <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1_nes_agent_reference_guide.md</code> (and fixes the Elfland <code>Mythril/Silver Sword</code> naming conflict to <code>Silver Sword</code>). We updated <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/lib/charts.js</code> and <code>lib/kgraph.js</code> to recognize all of the new columns and re-ran <code>jrpg-parser.js parse</code>, bringing <code>/home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/.robos/knowledge-graph.jsonld</code> to <strong>1,206 nodes</strong> with full combat, progression, magic, and encounter data.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run `python3 /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_resources.py` to decode the 6502 ROM binary tables into `~/Downloads/ff1/nes_data/*.json`",
