        {
          "id": "mod-05-godot-runtime-and-http-control-bridge",
          "title": "Module 5: Clean-Room Art Generation & Building the Godot 4 Runtime with Port 8090 Control",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "Generate clean-room sprites and backdrops with jrpg-parser.js plan/art/publish, run /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_godot_game.py to build the Godot 4.3 GL Compatibility game (TitleScreen, PartyCreate, FF1MapScene, GameState), and drive the game over HTTP via GameControlServer.gd on port 8090.",
          "lessonHtml": "\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">1. Generating Clean-Room Art (<code>jrpg-generate-art</code>) &amp; Publishing a Shareable Graph</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            When you want to share a parsed JRPG Knowledge Graph or ship a clean-room remake without any copyrighted reference rips, <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js</code> provides three commands driven by the <code>.agents/skills/jrpg-generate-art/SKILL.md</code> skill:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># 1. Inspect the 47 clean-room image generation jobs planned from the KGraph nodes\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js plan --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes\n\n# 2. Generate clean-room sprites, battle backdrops, and maps via robos-lib/ai-image (agy CLI) + tools/nesify.py\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js art --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes --limit 3\n\n# 3. Export a redistributable graph that strips all local file paths, SHA-256 hashes, and verbatim quotes\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js publish \\\n  --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes \\\n  --to /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes-public</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Under the hood, <code>lib/art.js</code> calls <code>packages/robos-lib/ai-image.js</code> (which invokes Gemini image generation through your signed-in Antigravity <code>agy</code> CLI) and then passes each raw image through <code>packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/nesify.py</code> to quantize colors to the 54-color NES hardware palette and remove background mattes. In our workspace, all 47 jobs (12 class sprites, 5 battle backdrops, 28 area maps, 1 world map, and 1 poster) are stored in <code>/home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes/assets/generated/</code>.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">2. How <code>build_ff1_godot_game.py</code> Constructs the Godot 4 Project</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            Next, <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_godot_game.py</code> scaffolds the playable Godot 4.3 (<code>gl_compatibility</code>) project inside <code>/home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game</code>. Every number in the game is loaded at runtime from the JSON tables we extracted in Modules 3 and 4:\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong><code>scripts/GameState.gd</code>:</strong> Autoload singleton that reads <code>res://data/classes_and_xp.json</code>, <code>res://data/enemies.json</code>, and <code>res://data/spells.json</code> on boot. Calling <code>reset_to_new_game()</code> constructs the 4 default Light Warriors—<code>ZEST</code> (Fighter, 35 HP, 20 STR, 10 DMG), <code>SAIL</code> (Thief, 30 HP, 58 EVA), <code>ROBO</code> (White Mage, 28 HP, 2 L1 MP, <code>CURE</code>), and <code>MAGE</code> (Black Mage, 25 HP, 2 L1 MP, <code>FIRE</code>)—with <code>400 Gold</code>.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong><code>scripts/TitleScreen.gd</code> &amp; <code>scripts/PartyCreate.gd</code>:</strong> Implements the NES blue-bordered prologue screen and the 4-character class/name creation screen, then transitions into <code>res://scenes/Coneria.tscn</code> at <code>Vector2i(16, 21)</code>.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            <strong><code>scripts/FF1MapScene.gd</code>:</strong> A single shared controller used by <code>Coneria.tscn</code>, <code>ConeriaCastle1F.tscn</code>, <code>ConeriaCastle2F.tscn</code>, and <code>TempleOfFiends.tscn</code>. It enforces tile walkability from the <code>64&times;64</code> grid, handles NPC dialogue (<code>TALK</code>) and treasure chest opening (<code>SEARCH</code>), and runs the turn-based NES battle engine when the party confronts Garland at <code>(19, 5)</code> in the Temple of Fiends.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-section\">\n          <h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">3. Why We Built <code>GameControlServer.gd</code> on Port 8090 Instead of Using X11 Keystrokes</h4>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            If you try to automate a game inside Xvfb by firing synthetic <code>xdotool</code> keystrokes on a wall-clock sleep timer and taking screenshots to guess whether a dialogue box opened, tests become slow and flaky. Instead, <code>scripts/GameControlServer.gd</code> runs as a Godot autoload singleton listening on <code>127.0.0.1:8090</code> using a non-blocking <code>TCPServer</code> polled inside <code>_process()</code>:\n          </p>\n          <pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code># REST API exposed by scripts/GameControlServer.gd on 127.0.0.1:8090:\nGET  /api/v1/health   -&gt; {\"status\": \"ok\", \"game\": \"Final Fantasy NES\", \"fps\": 60, \"port\": 8090}\nGET  /api/v1/state    -&gt; {\"scene_shown\": {\"name\": \"Coneria\", ...}, \"hero_position\": {\"x\": 16, \"y\": 18, \"facing\": \"up\"},\n                          \"dialog_box\": {\"visible\": false, \"last_text\": \"...\"}, \"battle\": {\"active\": false, ...},\n                          \"game_state\": {\"gold\": 400, \"inventory\": [...], \"garland_defeated\": false, \"party\": [...]}}\nPOST /api/v1/input    -&gt; {\"action\": \"ui_accept\" | \"ui_cancel\" | \"move_up\" | \"move_down\" | \"move_left\" | \"move_right\"}\nPOST /api/v1/command  -&gt; {\"command\": \"load_scene\" | \"teleport\" | \"select_command\", \"args\": {...}}</code></pre>\n          <p class=\"lesson-text\">\n            One critical Godot 4 operational rule: whenever you modify or re-render a PNG texture on disk (such as updating <code>assets/maps/temple_of_fiends.png</code> with the inside-room palette), you <strong>must</strong> run <code>godot4 --headless --path /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game --import</code> before launching the game. At runtime, Godot loads pre-imported binary <code>.godot/imported/*.ctex</code> files rather than decoding raw PNGs directly; running <code>--import</code> refreshes those <code>.ctex</code> caches.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      ",
          "labSteps": [
            "Run `jrpg-parser.js plan --out /home/ndipiazza/kgraphs/final-fantasy-nes` and inspect the 47 generated clean-room sprites, backdrops, and maps",
            "Run `python3 /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/build_ff1_godot_game.py` and `godot4 --headless --path /home/ndipiazza/Downloads/ff1/ff1-game --import`",
            "Inspect the JSON payload returned by `GameControlServer.gd` (`GET /api/v1/health` and `GET /api/v1/state`) on port `8090`",
            "Trace how `POST /api/v1/input` and `POST /api/v1/command` drive `TitleScreen.tscn` -> `PartyCreate.tscn` -> `Coneria.tscn`"
          ],
